---
# THE FLY TUNNEL -- what is left of the path Fly-hosted applications used to reach the database on.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, and it is the reason nix/modules/roles/wireguard-fly.nix publishes a
# metric rather than relying on anything built in: WIREGUARD IS SILENT BY DESIGN. There is no link
# state to watch. `ip link` reports wg0 UP whether or not anything is on the other end, because the
# interface is a virtual device that exists as soon as it is configured. A dead tunnel and an idle
# one look identical from every angle except one -- the age of the last completed handshake. That
# is what makes an unwatched WireGuard peer worth alerting on in the first place, and it is as true
# of a peer nothing currently uses as of one carrying the whole product.
#
# THE FAILURE IT WAS BUILT TO GUARD WAS THE QUIET KIND, and this is why the rules were written the
# way they are: while the applications were on Fly, a dropped tunnel left the web tier serving
# happily from its projected read model -- health checks green, pages rendering -- while the change
# stream was gone and the worker's writes failed, so the site quietly served an ageing corpus with
# nothing on either side changing state to say so.
#
# WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-08-29, AND WHY EVERY RULE HERE IS KEPT ANYWAY. Both tiers moved off Fly onto
# k3s (movies-gitops/, docs/domain-cutover.md), and they reach mongo-1 at its Hetzner PRIVATE
# address -- `10.20.0.10:27017`, one hop across ens10, see movies-gitops/worker/README.md. So
# this tunnel is no longer the path between the product and its data; no application traffic goes
# down it at all. The rules stay because the peers stay (mongo-1's and monitoring-1's, each
# explained in its own host file), and an unmonitored tunnel is exactly the thing this file argues
# should not exist. READ A FIRING ALERT ACCORDINGLY: it now means the peer is broken, not that the
# site has stopped updating -- and the honest first question is whether that peer should still be
# there at all, rather than how to repair it.
#
# THE THREE STATES ARE KEPT APART DELIBERATELY, because they have different causes:
#   * a handshake that has gone STALE  -- the path broke (Fly gateway moved, UDP flow lost, peer
#     removed on Fly's side). The tunnel worked once.
#   * a handshake that has NEVER happened (the metric is exactly 0) -- the configuration is wrong:
#     a key mismatch, the wrong endpoint, or the peer was never created. It has never worked.
#   * the metric being ABSENT -- nothing is watching the tunnel at all.
# Collapsing them into one alert would send somebody to check the network when the answer is a
# wrong key, or to re-run `fly wireguard create` when the peer is fine and the path is not.
groups:
  - name: wireguard-fly
    rules:
      - alert: WireguardFlyHandshakeStale
        # `!= 0` EXCLUDES THE NEVER-HANDSHAKED CASE, which would otherwise satisfy this expression
        # spectacularly (time() - 0 is 56 years) and hide the rule below it behind a wrong diagnosis.
        expr: >-
          kinowo_wireguard_latest_handshake_timestamp_seconds != 0
            and time() - kinowo_wireguard_latest_handshake_timestamp_seconds > 300
        # FIVE MINUTES IN THE EXPRESSION, and a short `for:` on top. WireGuard re-handshakes about
        # every two minutes while traffic flows and the peer sends a keepalive every 25s, so five
        # minutes of silence is already well outside normal. The `for:` guards against the metric
        # being sampled at an unlucky moment rather than against the tunnel being briefly quiet.
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "The Fly WireGuard tunnel on {{ $labels.host }} has not handshaked for over 5 minutes"
          description: >-
            This peer's path into Fly's 6PN is down. SINCE THE 2026-08-29 CUTOVER THAT IS NOT A
            PRODUCT OUTAGE -- the web tier and the workers run on k3s and reach mongo-1 over the
            Hetzner private network, so the site keeps serving AND keeps updating with this tunnel
            dead. Check `wg show wg0` on the host and `fly wireguard list` for whether this peer
            still exists on Fly's side; a peer removed there presents exactly like a network fault
            here, and while the Fly org is being wound down that is the likeliest cause. Deleting
            the peer (and its `fleet.wireguardFly` block) is a legitimate resolution rather than a
            workaround -- see the note in the host file that declares it.

      - alert: WireguardFlyNeverHandshaked
        expr: kinowo_wireguard_latest_handshake_timestamp_seconds == 0
        # TEN MINUTES, which is long enough to cover a boot and a first handshake and short enough
        # that a build which was never going to work is caught the same morning.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "The Fly WireGuard peer on {{ $labels.host }} has never completed a handshake"
          description: >-
            This is a CONFIGURATION fault, not a path fault -- the tunnel has never worked, so
            nothing about it has "broken". The usual causes, in order: the private key here does
            not match the public key Fly holds for this peer (rotating one end alone does not
            error, it just stops handshaking), the endpoint is wrong or the peer was deleted with
            `fly wireguard remove`, or allowedIPs does not include Fly's fdaa::/48. Note that
            mongod will have started anyway and will be listening on the tunnel address, because
            net.ipv6.ip_nonlocal_bind is set deliberately -- a bound socket here is NOT evidence
            that the tunnel works.

      - alert: WireguardFlyMetricAbsent
        expr: absent(kinowo_wireguard_latest_handshake_timestamp_seconds)
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is publishing the Fly tunnel's handshake age"
          description: >-
            Both alerts above are comparisons and cannot fire without this series, so THE ONLY
            SIGNAL THIS FLEET HAS about the state of that tunnel is gone -- and since WireGuard
            shows nothing at the interface level, gone means invisible rather than merely stale.
            Check the wireguard-fly-metrics timer on the host, and that node_exporter's textfile
            collector is reading the directory it writes to. An absent series is not a healthy
            tunnel. If the peer was intentionally removed, remove these rules with it rather than
            leaving a permanently firing warning nobody intends to act on.
